WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:24.080
Surveys have found that eighty percent of people get this problem wrong.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.660
While you are thinking about it, I am going to present a similar problem.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.020
Give these problems a try and when you're ready keep watching the video for the solution.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.940
Many people think that answer to the first problem

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.460
is C) Cannot Be Determined.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.300
However, we need to work through the logical possibilities.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.680
While we do not know Bob's marital status,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.000
we should work out each case

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.980
to see if the condition is met.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.320
One case is that Bob is married.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.940
In that case, he is a married person

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.880
who's looking at Charlie, who is unmarried.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.480
So if Bob is married,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.120
the condition that a married person is looking at an unmarried person is true.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.500
What would happen if Bob is not married?

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:43.020
In that case, Alice, who's married, is looking at Bob, who's unmarried.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.900
So in this case, too, [it] would be true that a married person is looking at an unmarried person.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.580
So the correct answer choice is A) Yes

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.960
Although we don't know which married person is looking at an unmarried person,

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.680
we know that some married person is looking at an unmarried person.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.600
Now let's tackle the next problem!

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.940
Imagine that we draw the three socks in order.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.820
Now, it could be the case that the first two socks you pull

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.820
are both black or they're both white.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.220
In that case, you would already have a matching pair of socks

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.980
before you even pull the third sock.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.680
Now what would happen if that's not the case?

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.160
Well, then the first two socks would have to be different colors.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.940
You would have to have one white and one black sock.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.640
Then, when you pull the third sock,

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.100
It would have to be either a white sock

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.960
--matching the white sock you already have--

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.420
or a black sock--matching the black sock you already have.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.100
The third sock must match one of the socks you already have.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:49.780
Therefore, in either case, you will always be holding a matching pair of socks.

